Use a project settings file while its content stays the same

snouty uses a project settings file (./.snouty.toml) only after you trust it.
Until you trust or deny the file, each command that reads the settings fails.

Records that you trust the settings file at PATH with its current content. When
the content changes, you must trust the file again.

A project settings file can set any setting, at the top level or in a profile,
including base_url, https_proxy and repository. snouty sends your API key to
base_url, sends its requests through https_proxy, and pushes your images to
repository, so a settings file from a cloned repository could send your
credentials or images to another server. Read the whole file, with every profile
in it, before you trust it. If you do not want snouty to use the file, run
`snouty settings deny`.

snouty trusts a file that you name with --settings or SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH, and
the global settings file. Set SNOUTY_SETTINGS_TRUST=all to use every project
settings file without a check, for example in CI.

snouty keeps the record in trust.toml, in the global settings directory.

Examples:
  snouty settings trust
  snouty settings trust path/to/.snouty.toml

Usage: snouty settings trust [OPTIONS] [PATH]

Arguments:
  [PATH]
          The project settings file
          
          [default: .snouty.toml]

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

      --json
          Output JSON where supported (NDJSON for list/stream commands, pretty
          JSON otherwise)

      --utc
          Show times in UTC instead of the local timezone

      --verbose
          Log API requests to stderr (authentication tokens redacted)

      --settings <SETTINGS>
          Path to the snouty settings file (default: ./.snouty.toml; overrides
          SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH)

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Settings profile to select (overrides ANTITHESIS_PROFILE)
